1202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 339 0 R
/T (The database design of the proposed B.E.A.C.O.N system is structured to efficiently manage user-related data, system-generated alerts, and application-level interactions. The database plays a crucial role in supporting functionalities such as user management, emergency communication, and system notifications. Each entity in the system is represented using structured tables with clearly defined attributes and relationships.)
/E (The database design of the proposed B.E.A.C.O.N system is structured to efficiently manage user-related data, system-generated alerts, and application-level interactions. The database plays a crucial role in supporting functionalities such as user management, emergency communication, and system notifications. Each entity in the system is represented using structured tables with clearly defined attributes and relationships.)
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 339 0 R
/T (The primary table in the database is the user table, which stores essential information such as user ID, name, email, and mobile number. This table acts as the central entity, with other tables linked to it using foreign keys. Additional tables are used to manage alerts, messages, and reminders generated during system operation. Relationships between tables are maintained using constraints such as primary keys, foreign keys, and unique attributes to ensure data integrity.)
/E (The primary table in the database is the user table, which stores essential information such as user ID, name, email, and mobile number. This table acts as the central entity, with other tables linked to it using foreign keys. Additional tables are used to manage alerts, messages, and reminders generated during system operation. Relationships between tables are maintained using constraints such as primary keys, foreign keys, and unique attributes to ensure data integrity.)
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1204 0 obj
<<
